"last_trial\" could be the index of the trial where that entry was last updated.\n\nLet's examine the fixtures. The first case has capacity 3, 7 trials. Signatures: e1, e3, e0, e1, e3, e1, e0. So e1 appears 3 times, e3 appears 3 times, e0 appears 2 times. With capacity 3, the memory might keep only 3 entries. Which ones? Possibly the ones with highest last_trial? Or the most recent reflections for each signature? But if capacity is total, then only 3 entries out of 7. Could be the last 3 distinct signatures? Not sure.\n\nThe task might be a known thing: \"Reflection Memory\" from some AI agent framework. Possibly it's about storing reflections for each signature, but keeping only the most recent one per signature? No, that would produce at most 3 entries (if 3 distinct signatures) but capacity is 3. But the output memory array could have multiple entries per signature? The schema allows it.\n\nMaybe we need to simulate a fixed-capacity memory that stores the most recent trials, but when full, evicts the least recent? The \"last_trial\" field might be the trial index of the last time that entry was referenced. That sounds like an LRU cache. Indeed, a common pattern: maintain a cache of items with a capacity, each item has a key (signature), and when we encounter a trial, we update the entry for that signature (if exists) or add a new one. If capacity is reached, we remove the least recently used (lowest last_trial). The \"last_trial\" is the index of the trial when that item was last used. The \"reflection\" and \"success\" are from the trial. The \"signatures\" array might be the list of signatures in the memory in order of last use? Or just all signatures that ever appeared? The output \"signatures\" is an array of strings. Could be the list of distinct signatures in the memory at the end, in some order.\n\nLet's test with capacity=3 and trials order. Suppose we process trials in order, index 0 to 6. For each trial, we update the memory entry for that signature with the trial's reflection and success, and set last_trial to current index. Initially empty. After all trials, memory will contain up to 3 entries, each with the most recent reflection for that signature? But if a signature appears later, its entry is updated. So the final memory would contain the last update for each signature that is still in memory. Which ones stay? If capacity is 3, and there are 3 distinct signatures, all might stay, but we need to see eviction. In the first case, there are 3 distinct signatures: e0, e1, e3. So if we keep only one per signature, we would have 3 entries, one for each, with last_trial being the last occurrence: e0 last at index 6, e1 at index 5, e3 at index 4. That would fit capacity 3. The memory would have entries for e0 (last_trial=6, reflection=r6, success=False), e1 (last_trial=5, reflection=r5, success=True), e3 (last_trial=4, reflection=r4, success=False). The \"signatures\" array might be the keys in order of last_trial descending? Or maybe the order they were added? Not specified.\n\nBut wait, what if capacity is smaller than number of distinct signatures? In second case, capacity=3, distinct signatures: e1, e2, e3 (3 distinct). Still fits. Third case: capacity=3, distinct signatures: e0, e1, e3 (3 distinct). So all cases have exactly 3 distinct signatures, capacity=3. That's too convenient. Possibly the challenge is exactly about LRU cache. The output schema includes \"memory\" as an array, not a dict, so order matters. The order might be the order of last use (LRU order) with most recent at end? Or from least recent to most recent? It's ambiguous.\n\nLet's look at the \"signatures\" array in output. It's a standalone array. Might be just the list of signatures that appear in memory, maybe sorted by something. Or it could be the list of all unique signatures that have ever appeared? But then why have a separate field? The schema allows any order, but we must ensure it's a list of strings.\n\nGiven the task is from \"effectslice\" and the specification is detailed, we need to produce a correct implementation. Since we don't have expected outputs, we must rely on typical interpretation. The task description: \"Registered procedure task AGENT-RF-01\" and \"Return one JSON object...\". The function must not be trivial; it likely implements a specific algorithm. The key hint: \"capacity\" and \"trials\" with \"signature\", \"reflection\", \"success\". This resembles a cache with limited capacity that stores the most recent reflection for each signature, evicting the least recently used when full. The memory objects have \"last_trial\" which is the index of the trial when that entry was last updated. The output should reflect the state of the cache after processing all trials.\n\nWe need to store the entries in the order of last use, possibly least recent first (LRU order) or most recent first. The LRU cache typically keeps the order of access. In Python, we can use a dict to maintain key-value pairs and a list for order. But we need to output an array of objects for memory. The objects must have last_trial, reflection, signature, success. The \"memory\" array should contain one entry per signature currently in cache. The \"signatures\" array might be the same as the keys in memory, but maybe it's the list of signatures in the order they were first added? Or the order of last access? It's not clear.\n\nGiven the symmetric simplicity, I'll assume:\n- Process trials in order.\n- Maintain a dictionary that maps signature to an object with reflection, success, last_trial (trial index).\n- Also maintain an ordered list of signatures (by last use) to implement LRU eviction when capacity is exceeded. When a signature is accessed (trial), we move it to the end (most recent).\n- After all trials, the memory is the list of entries in the order of last use from least recent to most recent (i.e., the order in the LRU list). Or we could output in any order but the schema doesn't specify. However, for